How code says that something cannot or must not happen, and where it is written.

| Name | Use |
|---|---|
| `ca::trap(reason)` | stop here: a broken invariant, an out-of-bounds index, an absent value read |
| `ca::assume(cond)` | promise the optimizer cond holds; undefined if it does not |
| `ca::unreachable()` | promise this point is never reached; undefined if it is |
| `ca::is_constant_folded(v)` | whether the optimizer proved v constant at this call site |
| `ca::source_location` | file, function and line of a call, for error traces |

### Traps and contexts

ca has no exceptions. An operation that cannot fail in a correct program (operators,
`operator[]`, reading an optional that holds nothing) traps instead of returning an error. A
trap outside every context ends the program with an illegal instruction, so a debugger stops
right on it. Inside a context (`ca::with`, `ca::context_manager`, layer 4) it unwinds to the
context instead, which then fails with the reason as its error message: a server can turn a
bug in one request into a failed request.

```c++
#include "ca/debug.hpp"
#include "ca/trace.hpp"
#include "ca/with.hpp"

[[gnu::noinline]] static auto check_positive(const int value) noexcept -> int {
	if (value <= 0)
		ca::trap("value must be positive");
	return value;
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	int seen = 0;
	auto done = ca::with([&seen](void) noexcept { seen = check_positive(-1); },
		[](const ca::error*) noexcept {});
	if (done || seen != 0)
		return 1;
	return ca::trace::of(done.error()).frames()[0].message() == ca::sv::str{"value must be positive"} ? 0 : 2;
}
```

The reason must outlive the context, so it is a string literal. The frames between the trap and
the context are left without running destructors: what they owned leaks. Traps are for bugs,
not for expected failures, which return `ca::result`.

The machinery is `recovery.hpp`: each context pushes an `impl::recovery` point (a
`__builtin_setjmp` buffer) on a per-thread list, and `ca::trap` jumps to the innermost one.
Nothing below layer 4 manipulates the list.

### Optimizer hints

`assume` and `unreachable` let the compiler drop checks it cannot prove useless. They are
promises, not checks: when they are wrong the behavior is undefined, so they belong where a
preceding check or an invariant makes them true.

```c++
#include "ca/debug.hpp"

enum class shape { circle, square };

[[nodiscard]] constexpr auto corners(const shape s) noexcept -> int {
	switch (s) {
		case shape::circle:
			return 0;
		case shape::square:
			return 4;
	}
	ca::unreachable();
}

[[nodiscard]] inline auto halve(const unsigned value) noexcept -> unsigned {
	ca::assume(value % 2U == 0U);
	return value / 2U;
}

static_assert(corners(shape::square) == 4);
```

`is_constant_folded(v)` is `__builtin_constant_p`, forced inline so the answer belongs to the
caller after inlining: a function can pick a branch-free path for a constant argument and a
general one otherwise. It is always false at -O0.

### Source locations

A `ca::source_location` parameter defaulted to `current()` records where the function was
called, not where it is declared, with no macro. The strings are literals of static storage.

```c++
#include "ca/debug.hpp"
#include "ca/integers.hpp"

static auto called_from(const ca::source_location where = ca::source_location::current()) noexcept -> ca::u32 {
	return where.line();
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	const ca::u32 here = __LINE__;
	return called_from() == here + 1U ? 0 : 1;
}
```
